            "isNew": false,
            "frameCount": 17,
            "promptText": "Use the Pantopus house style pasted above and the Foundations components from prompt 00, by exact name.\n\nSCREEN: What neighbors see · f9-privacy-mirror\n\nTYPE: EXTENSION of the existing designed screen “What neighbors see”. This screen already exists in the Pantopus design system. The attached screenshot is exact. Keep everything and change only what is listed.\n1. Add a place selector to the header, so the screen opens for anyone with a saved place, not only for someone with a claimed home.\n2. Add a new row, “Where your posts appear”, drawn with PublicPointMap.\n3. Add a new row, “Hidden from neighbors”.\n4. Show the same one-line promise inline in the Nearby post composer’s location picker.\nKeep the existing Address and Profile rows, their controls and their order.\n\nATTACH: “What neighbors see” as it looks today on web 1440, web 390, iOS and Android · the Place file showing its “What neighbors see” row · the Place dashboard privacy row · the Nearby post composer with its location picker open (iOS and web 390) · one Nearby feed card, because its header is quoted on this screen.\n\nPLATFORMS & VIEWPORTS: Web 1440x900 (left sidebar, content column as in the screenshot) and 390x844 (bottom tab bar). iOS 393x852: a pushed screen, with the composer as a sheet with detents. Android 412x915: Material 3 top app bar, with the composer as a bottom sheet.\n\nWHERE IT LIVES & HOW PEOPLE ARRIVE: Place tab → Place file → row “What neighbors see”. People also arrive from:\n- the privacy row on the Place tab, as in the second attached Place screenshot;\n- the Nearby tab → new post → location picker. The picker shows only the one-liner; its link expands a small map inside the picker.\n- deep links. The web link /app/homes/:id/privacy and the iOS link pantopus://homes/:id/privacy open with that home selected (iOS handling is an assumption; see Notes). Android needs a new route for pantopus://homes/:id/privacy (see Notes); draw the landing as if it exists.\nThe previous screen hands over one place (a home or a saved place), and the selector opens on it. No journey in the flow spec passes through this screen, so it must make sense on its own. Back returns to wherever the person came from.\n\nWHO AND WHEN: It is Mon 19 Oct 2026, 6:10 PM. Maya Chen at HOME A has 7 public Nearby posts and wants to check that neighbors can’t tell which house is hers. The second case is Jordan Lee at PLACE B: saved on Sat 10 Oct, not claimed, and about to post for the first time. Before this change, the screen never opened for Jordan.\n\nTHE ONE JOB: Let anyone who posts, including someone with only a saved place, see exactly what a neighbor learns about their location, drawn to true scale so they can check it.\n\nFIRST FIVE SECONDS: First the selector (which place, and who sees it), second the map pin labelled “What neighbors see”, third the distance caption under the map. On web 390 and phones, the map’s top edge must be visible without scrolling in frames 2, 3 and 7. The screen is proof, so there is no primary button. Each row keeps its one control on the same row, and the most prominent is “Manage where your posts appear”.\n\nCONTENT (house style fixtures; only the deltas are listed)\n- Title: “What neighbors see”.\n- Header selector. It shows the place label without a house number, so no frame ties the public spot to a full address. Maya: “Larkspur Loop · Vancouver” plus a static ScopeChip “Your household”, with a chevron. Maya’s other place is a saved place, shown as “Mom’s place near the lake · Camas” with a static ScopeChip “Saved place · Only you” (invented; list on Notes). Jordan: “Birchfield Ct · Camas” plus a static ScopeChip “Saved place · Only you”, no chevron, no other places.\n- Full addresses appear only inside the open “Show for” menu (frame 4): “2418 NE Larkspur Loop, Vancouver”; “Mom’s place near the lake” above “612 NE Leadbetter Rd, Camas”.\n- Caption under the selector: “What a neighbor learns about you from this place. Your posts are below.”\n- Address row: “A neighbor sees: Vancouver, WA” (HOME A) or “A neighbor sees: Camas, WA” (PLACE B and Maya’s saved place), with the control “Change”.\n- Profile row: “A neighbor sees: Maya C. · Joined Aug 2026” or “A neighbor sees: Jordan L. · Joined Oct 2026”, with the control “Edit profile”.\n- Where your posts appear, in this order:\n  - the change note “Since Mon 12 Oct, neighbors get only this spot, never your exact location.”;\n  - PublicPointMap;\n  - the caption. Home: “Neighbors see your posts at this spot, the same spot every time, about 0.3 mi from your address.” Saved place: “Neighbors see your posts at this spot, the same spot every time, about 0.3 mi from this address.”\n  - the label “Your post’s header, as a neighbor sees it:”, followed by the header exactly as the attached Nearby feed card draws it: “Maya C. · Vancouver, WA · 2h” (HOME A), “Maya C. · Camas, WA · 2h” (Maya’s saved place), “Jordan L. · Camas, WA · 2h” (PLACE B). The location part is always the city, never a street.\n  - “Your public posts: 7” with the link “Manage where your posts appear”.\n- Hidden from neighbors. Home: “Your street address · Your home on the map”. Saved place: “Your street address · This place’s exact spot on the map”.\n- A collapsed disclosure, “How this spot is chosen”, with three lines:\n  1. “We move your post’s location by a fixed amount before anyone else sees it.”\n  2. “Every post uses the same spot, so posting more doesn’t narrow down where you live.”\n  3. “It’s the same spot on the Nearby feed, the map, saved posts and each post’s page.”\n  Lines 2 and 3 are gated by engineering checks (see Notes); draw all three.\n- Composer picker, under the chosen place: PLACE B “Neighbors see “Camas, WA” and a spot about 0.3 mi away, never your address.” HOME A “Neighbors see “Vancouver, WA” and a spot about 0.3 mi away, never your address.” Then the link “See what neighbors see”.\n- No posts (Jordan): “Your public posts: 0”. The caption becomes “When you post, neighbors will see it at this spot, the same spot every time, about 0.3 mi from this address.”\n- Worst case (stress values, not a real account state): Maya’s saved place label wrapping to two lines, 6 places in the selector (HOME A, Mom’s place and four more saved places you invent and list on Notes), “Your public posts: 1,248”, and “about 0.4 mi”, the largest offset the server allows.\n\nLAYOUT & VISUALIZATION: Use one column of the existing cards. Each row has a label, then “A neighbor sees:” and the value in body type, then its control right-aligned on the same row. The selector row (label, static ScopeChip and chevron together) is one full-width 44pt target.\n\nDraw the posts row with PublicPointMap (privacy mirror variant):\n- A 16:9 map at full card width.\n- The map is centred exactly on the public pin, never on the address or a midpoint. Zoom keeps the pin centred, and panning starts from the pin, so no frame’s centre, crop or zoom level encodes where the address is.\n- Take street labels only from the streets around the pin: NE 28th St, NE 164th Ave and NE 23rd St for HOME A; NE 3rd Ave, NE Everett St and NE Lake Rd for PLACE B; NE Lake Rd, NE 232nd Ave and SE Crown Rd for Maya’s saved place. Never label or emphasise the street the address is on (Larkspur Loop, Birchfield Ct, Leadbetter Rd), even if it falls inside the map. No street gets special styling.\n- Exactly one pin, in primary.700, with the label “What neighbors see” on a surface.base plate.\n- The address is unmarked: no home icon, ring, box, halo or dot anywhere.\n- Nothing is drawn on top of the map except the pin and its label.\n- Directly below the map, a control row: the scale bar on the left (“0 · 0.1 · 0.2 · 0.3 · 0.4 mi”), then on the right the buttons Zoom out, Zoom in, and a four-arrow pan pad drawn as one row of four buttons. Each button is 44pt on iOS, 48dp on Android and 44px on web, with 8pt gaps. At 390 and on phones, if the row does not fit, the pan pad wraps to a second row under the zoom buttons.\n\nThe pin is the spot a neighbor’s Nearby map actually shows, so the picture is the audience’s view, not a diagram of how it works. The quoted header sits directly under the caption.\n\nFallbacks:\n- If the tile fails, keep the pin, the label, the scale bar and the control row, and draw the nearby streets as plain labelled vector lines, under the same labelling rule. Never replace the map with a paragraph.\n- In the composer, the link expands the picker row in place to show a small PublicPointMap (composer one-liner variant), centred on the pin: zoom keeps the pin centred, and the map does not pan. Do not open a second sheet.\n\nINTERACTION, MOTION & HAPTICS\n- Tapping the selector row opens a menu titled “Show for”: a pull-down menu on iOS, a dropdown menu on Android, a listbox popover on web. Each place in it shows its full address and its ScopeChip. The ScopeChip is a static label everywhere and never a separate button.\n- Picking a place redraws the rows in place with a 200ms cross-fade. Show no skeleton if the data is cached.\n- The map pans and zooms with the buttons. Drag and pinch are optional extras, never the only way.\n- The disclosure expands in place and its chevron rotates over 150ms.\n- Under Reduce Motion, every change is a cross-fade and nothing moves.\n- “Change” and “Edit profile” open their existing screens. “Manage where your posts appear” opens the existing post-location control in Nearby (the feed’s location picker).\n- A stale deep link shows the LandingBannerSlot line at the top with the trailing action “Choose a place”, which opens the “Show for” menu, and opens the first place.\n- Retry refetches only its slot, in place.\n- No haptics, beyond the system menu tick on iOS.\n\nFOUNDATIONS COMPONENTS USED: PublicPointMap (privacy mirror variant; composer one-liner variant) · ScopeChip (“Saved place · Only you” / “Your household”, static in every case) · LandingBannerSlot (proposed stale-place-link variant with trailing action “Choose a place”; see Notes) · WarmingSkeleton (map box, control row and row bars) · OfflineNotice with FreshnessLine (offline variant) · InlineErrorRow (provider-unreachable variant).\n\nACCESSIBILITY\n- Reading order: title → selector (place label, then scope) → selector caption → Address → Profile → change note → map → control row → caption → quoted header → count → link → Hidden → disclosure.\n- The selector row is one button: “Showing Larkspur Loop, Vancouver. Visible to your household. Show for another place.” For Jordan it is not a button and reads “Birchfield Ct, Camas. Visible to only you.”\n- The ScopeChip alone is read as “Visible to only you” or “Visible to your household”, with no hint.\n- The map is one element. Spoken label, home: “Map. Neighbors see your posts at one spot about 0.3 miles from your address, near NE 164th Ave and NE 28th St. Your home is not shown. Scale in tenths of a mile.” Saved place: “Map. Neighbors see your posts at one spot about 0.3 miles from this address, near NE 3rd Ave and NE Everett St. This place’s exact spot is not shown. Scale in tenths of a mile.”\n- Spoken labels for the map buttons: “Zoom in”, “Zoom out”, “Move map north”, “Move map south”, “Move map east”, “Move map west”.\n- Retry spoken labels: “Retry map of where your posts appear” (map slot) and “Retry what neighbors see” (whole screen).\n- The quoted header is read as “A neighbor sees: Maya C., Vancouver, Washington, 2 hours ago.”\n- The pin is told apart by its shape and its printed label, not by colour.\n- Targets are 44pt on iOS, 48dp on Android with 8dp gaps, and 44px on web.\n- The caption and the Hidden row together are the text alternative to the map.\n- At AX5, controls stack under their values, the selector label wraps, and the map control row wraps.\n\nCOPY: Use every string above, plus these:\n- Offline: “You’re offline · as of 5:52 PM”. Under each disabled control: “Changing this needs a connection.”\n- Map failed: “We couldn’t load where your posts appear. Retry”.\n- Screen failed: “We couldn’t load what neighbors see. Retry”.\n- Stale deep link: “That home isn’t on your account anymore. Showing Larkspur Loop.” with the action “Choose a place”.\n\nEDGE CASES\n- Long labels wrap to two lines and never truncate.\n- With 6 places, the menu scrolls.\n- 1,248 posts shows with a thousands comma; 0.4 mi is the largest distance ever printed.\n- Loads under 1s show nothing. Cold loads show WarmingSkeleton at the final heights.\n- Offline, the cached pin stays and all three controls are disabled, each with its reason; “Choose a place” is disabled with the same reason.\n- Someone with only one place (Jordan) sees the selector as a plain header with no chevron, and it is not a button.\n- A saved place never shows the words home, household or claim.\n- A claimed home shows “Your household”.\n- A household member (Sam) sees his own profile row.\n- Every frame except the open menu (frame 4) is safe to screenshot: none shows a house number next to the pin, and none centres, labels or emphasises the address.\n\nINSTEAD OF\n- Instead of a ring or box centred on the address, draw one pin at the public spot labelled “What neighbors see”, with the map centred on that pin — because any shape or frame centred on the home gives the home away in a screenshot.\n- Instead of “re-rolled every time you post”, write “the same spot every time” — because the server keeps one stable offset, and fresh offsets could be averaged to find the home.\n- Instead of a 150 m ring with a “500 ft” bar, draw a scale bar in tenths of a mile — because the real offset reaches about 0.4 mi and the picture must match the server.\n- Instead of explaining the protection in prose, show the neighbor’s literal header and pin with each control on its row — because audience views raised correct answers from 66% to 94% (Lipford 2008).\n- Instead of a shield, score or “You’re protected” graphic, draw the “Hidden from neighbors” row — because a checkable fact beats reassurance.\n- Instead of gating the screen on a claimed home, draw the selector — because saved-place users are the people posting from a saved place.\n- Instead of the full street address in the header, draw the place label without a house number — because the address next to a stable pin links every future post to the home.\n- Instead of zoom and pan buttons floating on the map, draw them in a row under it — because at phone width they would cover the pin; and instead of an empty or prose-only map when tiles fail, draw vector streets with the same pin and scale bar.\n\nDONE WHEN\n- Jordan, with only a saved place, can open the screen.\n- Maya can point to the pin, read the streets around it, and judge the distance with the scale bar.\n- No screenshot-safe frame shows or implies where the home is: the map is centred on the pin, the address street is unlabelled, and no house number appears.\n- The composer one-liner and the mirror say the same thing: the city and a spot about 0.3 mi away.\n- The quoted header matches the attached feed card, with the city and never a street.\n- “Saved place · Only you” or “Your household” is stated in the header.\n- Every map action has a button, and nothing covers the pin.\n- The words “re-rolled” appear nowhere.\n\nARTBOARDS\n1. f9-privacy-mirror · web-1440 · 01-home-dense · light: Maya, “Larkspur Loop · Vancouver”, “Your household”, Vancouver, WA, about 0.3 mi, 7 posts, disclosure collapsed, selector with chevron.\n2. f9-privacy-mirror · ios · 02-saved-place-first-post · light: Jordan, “Birchfield Ct · Camas”, “Saved place · Only you”, Camas, WA, 0 posts, future-tense caption, pin still drawn, map top edge visible without scrolling.\n3. f9-privacy-mirror · web-390 · 03-saved-place-only · light: Jordan at 390, plain header with no chevron, map control row wrapped, map top edge visible without scrolling.\n4. f9-privacy-mirror · android · 04-selector-open · light: Maya’s “Show for” menu with both full addresses, each with its ScopeChip; a label above the frame (not in the UI): “The open menu is not part of the screenshot-safe view.”\n5. f9-privacy-mirror · ios · 05-how-spot-chosen-open · light: Maya, the disclosure expanded with all three lines.\n6. f9-privacy-mirror · ios · 06-composer-picker · light: Jordan’s Nearby composer sheet with the one-liner and the small map expanded; Close visible.\n7. f9-privacy-mirror · android · 07-saved-place-selected · light: Maya with “Mom’s place near the lake · Camas” selected; saved-place copy in the Hidden row and caption, about 0.3 mi; map top edge visible without scrolling.\n8. f9-privacy-mirror · web-390 · 08-loading · light: WarmingSkeleton holding the 16:9 map box, the control row and the row bars.\n9. f9-privacy-mirror · web-390 · 09-error · light: the map slot shows InlineErrorRow; the Address and Profile rows stay. Inset A: the whole screen failed, with “We couldn’t load what neighbors see. Retry”. Inset B: the LandingBannerSlot stale-link line with “Choose a place” over Larkspur Loop.\n10. f9-privacy-mirror · android · 10-offline-cached · light: Maya, the cached pin, FreshnessLine, three disabled controls with reasons.\n11. f9-privacy-mirror · web-390 · 11-tile-failed-vector · light: vector streets with the same pin, scale bar and control row.\n12. f9-privacy-mirror · web-1440 · 12-worst-case · light: the two-line label, 6 places, 1,248 posts, 0.4 mi.\n13. f9-privacy-mirror · ios · 13-ax5 · light: Maya at AX5, controls stacked, selector label wrapped.\n14. f9-privacy-mirror · web-390 · 14-greyscale · light: frame 3 drawn entirely in greyscale.\n15. f9-privacy-mirror · web-1440 · 15-home-dense · dark: the dark twin of frame 1.\n16. f9-privacy-mirror · ios · 16-saved-place-first-post · dark: the dark twin of frame 2.\n17. f9-privacy-mirror · web-1440 · 17-notes · light: Notes.\n   - Invented strings: Maya C. · Joined Aug 2026; Jordan L. · Joined Oct 2026; Mon 12 Oct; “Mom’s place near the lake”; 612 NE Leadbetter Rd, Camas; “Birchfield Ct · Camas” and “Larkspur Loop · Vancouver” selector labels; the four extra worst-case places; all street names; 0.3/0.4 mi; 7 and 1,248 posts; 5:52 PM; “See what neighbors see”; the selector caption; the stale-link line and “Choose a place”; the selector-row spoken label; the Retry spoken labels.\n   - Fixture delta: Maya has a saved place in Camas that is not in the house style.\n   - Assumptions: the feed-card header format “Name · City, WA · time”; saved-place captions say “from this address” instead of the contract’s “from your address”; selector labels drop the house number; iOS already handles pantopus://homes/:id/privacy (engineering to confirm).\n   - Contract change requests: LandingBannerSlot gains a stale-place-link variant (no actor name first; trailing action “Choose a place”) and is used here outside Place and /start; ScopeChip is static here, with the selector row as the only target.\n   - Engineering: the mirror is built from the server’s own output for a pretend neighbor and never uses a neighbor’s sign-in. Distance and geometry come from the server’s stable offset (up to about ±0.005° each way), and engineering confirms them before the copy ships. Add a contract test that fails if the mirror’s offset constant differs from the server’s location-privacy constant. The mirror reflects each post’s location_precision: posts set to neighborhood_only or none are not at this spot; confirm the count and the copy cover them. Disclosure lines 2 and 3 ship only after that check, and line 3 only after the leak fix is verified on the feed, map, saved posts and the single-post API, including anonymous viewers. Android needs a route for pantopus://homes/:id/privacy.\n   - Open questions: Is the post count per place or per account? Does the posts row need a variant such as “2 posts show only your area, not a spot”? Does the composer ever use device location? What URL opens this screen for a saved place (not a home)? Should the disclosure also say that all your posts share one spot, so a neighbor can tell they come from the same person?\n   - Omitted states: web and Android composer, same as iOS frame 6. List any others.\n\nBATCH PLAN: Turn 1: artboards 1-6, then wait for “continue”. Turn 2: artboards 7-12, then wait for “continue”. Turn 3: artboards 13-17.",
            "changeLog": [
              "Map now centred exactly on the public pin; zoom and pan start from the pin (review major: home-centred framing leaks the home).",
              "Street labels are taken only from streets around the pin; the address street is never labelled or emphasised; DONE WHEN now reads 'read the streets around it' (two review majors; chose the leak-safe fix over adding Larkspur Loop).",
              "Selector and stale-link line show the place label without a house number; full addresses only in the open menu, which is marked as not screenshot-safe (review major).",
              "Composer one-liner now says neighbors see the city and a spot about 0.3 mi away, matching the mirror (review major, audience-view honesty).",
              "Notes engineering now covers location_precision (neighborhood_only, none); disclosure line 2 gated with line 3; open question on a posts-row variant (review major).",
              "ScopeChip made a static label; the whole selector row is the only 'Show for' target; chip-opens-menu assumption removed (review minor).",
              "FIRST FIVE SECONDS rewritten to selector, pin, caption, with map top edge visible on phones in frames 2, 3, 7 (review minor).",
              "Selector caption changed to 'What a neighbor learns about you from this place. Your posts are below.' (review minor).",
              "HOME A default distance set to 0.3 mi (per-surface-changes string); 0.4 mi kept only as the stress value (review minor).",
              "Frame 9 gains insets for the screen-failed state and the stale-link line; frame 14 is now frame 3 in greyscale (review minors).",
              "Retry spoken labels added for the map slot and the whole screen (contract InlineErrorRow).",
              "LandingBannerSlot stale-link line gains the trailing action 'Choose a place'; variant and out-of-scope use recorded as contract change requests on Notes (review minor, twice).",
              "iOS deep link handling listed as an assumption on Notes (review minor).",
              "Glossary: 'Place dashboard' kept only on the ATTACH line; 'bookmarked address' changed to 'saved place' (review minor).",
              "Maya's saved-place street labels changed to streets around the pin (NE Lake Rd, NE 232nd Ave, SE Crown Rd) so Leadbetter Rd is not labelled; listed as invented."
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "f9-curator-chip",
            "name": "From Pantopus chip + 'Why am I seeing this?'",
            "platforms": [
              "web",
              "ios",
              "android"
            ],
            "isNew": false,
            "frameCount": 18,
            "promptText": "Use the Pantopus house style pasted above and the Foundations components from prompt 00, by exact name.\n\nSCREEN: Nearby post card and post detail, Pantopus-authored variant · f9-curator-chip\n\nTYPE: EXTENSION of the existing designed screens “Nearby feed post card” and “Post detail”, plus the “Pulse card” (as in the attached screenshot) and the Nearby “map card” callout. The Pulse and map cards share this data but not the card component. This screen already exists in the Pantopus design system. The attached screenshot is exact. Keep everything and change only what is listed.\n1. On posts Pantopus wrote, a “From Pantopus” chip replaces the whole identity block.\n2. A SourceCaption line goes under the body.\n3. The overflow menu gains “Why am I seeing this?”, which expands in place. (The earlier half-sheet idea is replaced by this inline expansion.)\n4. The mute action writes to the existing Feed preferences.\nReport and neighbor posts are unchanged.\n\nATTACH: the Nearby feed with a neighbor card and a system card (web 1440, web 390, iOS, Android) · post detail with the overflow menu open (web and iOS) · the Pulse card in its current host screen · the Nearby map card callout · Feed preferences.\n\nPLATFORMS & VIEWPORTS: Web 1440x900 (left sidebar) and 390x844 (bottom tab bar). iOS 393x852: native list; overflow is a “…” button that opens a pull-down menu (UIMenu); long-press is not used. Android 412x915: Material 3 cards, a “⋮” button that opens a dropdown menu.\n\nWHERE IT LIVES & HOW PEOPLE ARRIVE: Nearby tab → feed card → post detail. The same variant appears on:\n- the Pulse card, in the host screen shown in its attached screenshot (host tab listed as an assumption on Notes);\n- the Nearby map card callout;\n- Saved posts;\n- a post opened from a notification deep link.\nTo open the explainer, a person taps “Why am I seeing this?” in the overflow menu, or taps the chip itself (hit area 44pt iOS / 48dp Android / 44px web). No journey in the flow spec hands anything to this card, so it must stand alone.\n\nWHO AND WHEN: It is Mon 19 Oct 2026, 6:10 PM. Maya Chen at HOME A scrolls Nearby after last night’s wind and sees a power-outage post. She wants to know whether a neighbor wrote it and, if not, whether she can hide posts like it.\n\nTHE ONE JOB: Let a reader tell at a glance that Pantopus, not a neighbor, posted this, and understand exactly what muting would hide.\n\nFIRST FIVE SECONDS: The eye lands first on the “From Pantopus” chip where a face would be, second on the body, and third on the Source line. Primary action: none on the card. Inside the explainer, the one action is the “Show Pantopus posts in Nearby” toggle.\n\nCONTENT (house style fixtures; only the deltas are listed). Draw a real feed with the three card kinds stacked, not one card alone.\n- Neighbor card, unchanged: avatar, “Marcus R. · Vancouver, WA · 4h”, “Anyone else lose power around 164th? Ours came back at 11.”, “6 replies”. The location part of a neighbor’s header is the city, as in the attached screenshot, never a street.\n- Pantopus card:\n  - identity slot: chip “From Pantopus” · “2h”;\n  - body: “Clark Public Utilities reports 412 customers without power near NE 164th Ave after last night’s wind. Their current estimate for power back is 8:30 PM tonight.” (The body may name a street because it describes a place, not a person’s home.)\n  - SourceCaption: “Source: Clark Public Utilities · outage map · as of 5:45 PM ↗”, linking to the publisher’s own outage page;\n  - overflow menu: “Report” · “Mute posts from Pantopus” · “Why am I seeing this?”.\n- System card: “Online or mail voter registration for Nov 3 must arrive by Oct 26 in Washington.” Keep its existing treatment.\n- Explainer: the heading “Why am I seeing this?”, then four lines:\n  1. “Pantopus shared this from a public source. No neighbor wrote it.”\n  2. “It came from Clark Public Utilities.”\n  3. “You’re seeing it because it’s about an area near your place in Vancouver.”\n  4. “It doesn’t count as neighborhood activity in Nearby.”\n  Then the toggle row “Show Pantopus posts in Nearby” (on) with the caption under it “Turning this off hides them in the Nearby feed and map. Posts you saved stay in Saved posts.”, the text button “Report this post”, and the text button “Show less” to collapse. Line 4 appears only once Pantopus posts are actually excluded from neighborhood counts. Draw a three-line inset beside the explainer (lines 1-3) to show the variant without it.\n- Post detail (densest version):\n  - a 16:9 photo captioned “Photo: Clark Public Utilities”;\n  - the headline “Crews working to restore power along NE 164th Ave and nearby streets after overnight wind”;\n  - topic chips “Power” · “Weather”;\n  - the SourceCaption line;\n  - the overflow menu open.\n- Worst case: the source “Washington State Department of Transportation” wraps to two lines; a 6-line body truncates with the existing “More”.\n\nLAYOUT & VISUALIZATION\nThe Pantopus row has no avatar-plus-name shape at all. The chip replaces the whole identity block: no avatar circle, no initials disc, no reserved 40px slot and no empty space where a face would be.\n\nChip:\n- ScopeChip geometry: pill, 24pt tall, hit area 44pt iOS / 48dp Android / 44px web;\n- fill surface.sunken, label in text.strong in label type;\n- a 12pt Pantopus mark glyph in text.secondary;\n- in dark mode, the dark raised fill and dark text primary;\n- no tick, star or badge glyph; it must not look like a verification badge;\n- sits on the byline baseline and reads as a label, not a person;\n- draw it so it cannot be confused with a selected topic chip (topic chips in post detail keep their own ChoiceChip look).\n\nThe Source line is SourceCaption in a proposed “post source” variant: no ProvenanceMark, because the post is Pantopus’s summary, not a confirmed fact; the external-link glyph trails it. It sits under the body. Keep it separate from the chip: who posted and where the fact came from are two different facts.\n\nThe explainer expands inline under the chip and pushes the body down. It is four lines plus a toggle row at least 44pt tall (48dp on Android), so it doesn’t warrant a sheet, and a sheet would cover the post being explained.\n\nVariants:\n- Pulse card: the chip takes the avatar position, left of the title.\n- Map card callout: the chip, a one-line title and the SourceCaption line.\n- Reported card: a persistent caption “Reported · reviewing” under the SourceCaption line.\n- No record of who posted (older cached copies): draw the body and the time only. Show no chip, no name, no placeholder identity, and no “Why am I seeing this?” item in its overflow menu.\n- Media failed: the image box keeps its 16:9 size and shows “Photo didn’t load · Retry”.\n- Cold load: one WarmingSkeleton row for a Pantopus card: a chip-width bar, body bars and the image box, with no avatar circle.\n\nINTERACTION, MOTION & HAPTICS\n- Tapping the chip or the overflow item toggles the explainer. It expands over 200ms and the chevron rotates. Under Reduce Motion it cross-fades. “Show less” collapses it; on web, Escape does the same as “Show less”.\n- Turning the toggle off saves to Feed preferences. What it hides: Pantopus posts in the Nearby feed and on the Nearby map. Saved posts keep them. Whether the Pulse card and the map callout are also filtered is an open question (Notes); draw them unchanged.\n- In the feed, after the mute: the card collapses in place to InlineUndo, and other Pantopus cards fade out of the feed behind it over 200ms. Focus moves to Undo. The InlineUndo row keeps the card’s scroll position; removed cards above it do not move it.\n- Choosing “Mute posts from Pantopus” in the overflow menu does the same as turning the toggle off.\n- On the Pulse card: the mute collapses that card in place to InlineUndo, with focus on Undo.\n- In post detail: the detail stays open. The toggle row is replaced in place by InlineUndo, focus moves to Undo, and the feed is filtered when the person goes back.\n- Undo stays until the person leaves the screen where the mute happened.\n- One light haptic tick when the mute is confirmed, and nothing else.\n- “Report” and “Report this post” open the existing report flow; on return the card shows “Reported · reviewing”.\n- Every action is a tap; nothing depends on swipe, long-press or hover.\n\nFOUNDATIONS COMPONENTS USED: SourceCaption (proposed “post source” variant: no ProvenanceMark, because the post is Pantopus’s summary, not a confirmed fact; external-link glyph; add to the Foundations board) · InlineUndo (removed-row variant: “Posts from Pantopus are hidden in Nearby · Undo”) · InlineErrorRow (save-failed variant, for the mute write) · OfflineNotice · WarmingSkeleton (row skeleton; chip bar and image box only, no avatar circle for Pantopus cards). The chip uses ScopeChip geometry only, and its text follows the glossary term “From Pantopus”.\n\nACCESSIBILITY\n- Card reading order: chip → time → body → Source → actions.\n- Chip: spoken label “From Pantopus, not a neighbor”, hint “Shows why you’re seeing this”, button trait, with an expanded or collapsed state (aria-expanded on web).\n- The source link is read as “Source: Clark Public Utilities, outage map, as of 5:45 PM, opens their website”.\n- The explainer is announced politely when it opens, and focus moves to its heading. “Show less” has the spoken label “Close explanation”; focus returns to the chip.\n- The toggle’s spoken label is “Show Pantopus posts in Nearby, on”, followed by its caption.\n- The mute confirmation and the error are live regions. After a mute, focus is on Undo.\n- Retry on the mute failure has the spoken label “Retry hiding posts from Pantopus”.\n- Targets: 44pt on iOS, 48dp on Android with 8dp gaps, 44px on web.\n- The chip is told apart by its shape, glyph and words, not colour.\n- At AX5, the chip and time stack, and the explainer lines wrap without truncating.\n\nCOPY: Use every string above, plus these:\n- “Turn them back on in Feed preferences.”\n- Mute failed: “We couldn’t hide posts from Pantopus. Your feed hasn’t changed. Retry”.\n- Reported: “You reported this post. We’ll review it by Mon 26 Oct (in 7 days) and tell you here.”\n- Offline, under the disabled toggle and under Report: “Changing this needs a connection.”\n- Already muted, when the post is opened from a link: the toggle shows off, with the caption “Pantopus posts are hidden in your Nearby feed.”\n\nEDGE CASES\n- Long source names wrap to two lines and never ellipsise.\n- A 6-line body truncates.\n- Many Pantopus posts in a row each carry the chip, and muting removes them all behind one undo.\n- Zero Pantopus posts: nothing changes.\n- Slow media: reserve the image box, never an avatar.\n- Offline: the toggle and Report are disabled, with reasons.\n- Rows with no author record show no chip and have no “Why am I seeing this?” item.\n- A post in Saved posts stays there after a mute and keeps its chip.\n- A household member and an owner see the same card.\n\nINSTEAD OF\n- Instead of an avatar, initials disc or logo circle, put the “From Pantopus” chip where the whole identity block goes — because an avatar-plus-name shape reads as a person.\n- Instead of a blue selected-style or ticked chip, draw a neutral sunken pill with the mark glyph — because a coloured chip reads as a selected filter or an endorsement badge.\n- Instead of “Pantopus curator”, write “From Pantopus” — because “curator” is internal vocabulary readers don’t know (NN/g heuristic 2).\n- Instead of a sheet or modal for the explanation, expand four lines in place — so the post being explained stays visible.\n- Instead of a collapse button called “Hide” next to a mute toggle, write “Show less” — because “Hide” would be mistaken for the mute.\n- Instead of a guessed byline when the app doesn’t know who posted, draw no identity at all — because a placeholder could make a Pantopus post look like a neighbor’s.\n- Instead of the source as a tooltip or chip, draw a SourceCaption linking to the publisher, with its as-of time — so a reader can check the fact itself.\n- Instead of a mute that only closes the menu, or line 4 before exclusion from counts ships, save the mute to Feed preferences with InlineUndo in the card’s place, and draw the three-line variant — because every line must be literally true.\n\nDONE WHEN\n- In the three-card frame, a reader can tell which card a neighbor wrote without reading the body.\n- The chip never reads as a person, a selected filter or a badge.\n- The explainer says who posted, where it came from and why this person sees it; every line is literally true and comes with its control.\n- The reader can tell from the toggle caption exactly what muting hides and what it keeps.\n- Mute takes one tap, from the toggle or the overflow item, with undo, focus on Undo and a failure path.\n- Cached rows with no record of who posted show neither a chip, a byline nor a “Why” item.\n- The Pulse and map variants match the card.\n- No neighbor header shows a street.\n\nARTBOARDS\n1. f9-curator-chip · web-1440 · 01-feed-three-kinds · light: neighbor, Pantopus and system cards stacked.\n2. f9-curator-chip · ios · 02-feed-collapsed · light: the same feed on iOS, explainer collapsed, “…” overflow button visible.\n3. f9-curator-chip · ios · 03-why-expanded · light: the four lines, the toggle and its caption, Report this post, Show less, and the three-line inset.\n4. f9-curator-chip · android · 04-muted-undo · light: InlineUndo in the card’s place with focus ring on Undo, the feed re-filtered behind it.\n5. f9-curator-chip · web-1440 · 05-post-detail-overflow · light: photo, headline, topic chips, SourceCaption, overflow menu open.\n6. f9-curator-chip · web-390 · 06-feed-three-kinds · light: the three cards at 390.\n7. f9-curator-chip · android · 07-no-author-record · light: a cached row with no chip, no byline, and its overflow menu open without “Why am I seeing this?”.\n8. f9-curator-chip · ios · 08-mute-failed · light: InlineErrorRow, toggle back on.\n9. f9-curator-chip · web-390 · 09-reported-and-already-muted · light: the reported card with its review line and the persistent “Reported · reviewing” caption, and a linked post with the toggle off and its caption.\n10. f9-curator-chip · android · 10-loading-and-media-failed · light: one WarmingSkeleton Pantopus row (chip bar, body bars, image box, no avatar), one image box loading, one failed with Retry.\n11. f9-curator-chip · ios · 11-pulse-and-map-variants · light: stacked: the Pulse card in its host context (as in the attached screenshot) above the Nearby map callout over its map.\n12. f9-curator-chip · android · 12-offline · light: OfflineNotice, toggle and Report disabled with reasons.\n13. f9-curator-chip · web-1440 · 13-worst-case · light: the long source name, a truncated body, three Pantopus cards in a row.\n14. f9-curator-chip · ios · 14-ax5 · light: frame 3 at AX5.\n15. f9-curator-chip · web-1440 · 15-greyscale · light: frame 1 in greyscale.\n16. f9-curator-chip · web-1440 · 16-feed-three-kinds · dark: the dark twin of frame 1.\n17. f9-curator-chip · ios · 17-why-expanded · dark: the dark twin of frame 3.\n18. f9-curator-chip · web-1440 · 18-notes · light: Notes.\n   - Invented strings: Marcus R., his post and “6 replies”, the outage body, 412, 8:30 PM, “outage map”, 5:45 PM, the headline, the photo caption, the topic chips, Mon 26 Oct review date, “Reported · reviewing”, the WSDOT example, “Report this post”, “Show less”, “Close explanation”, the toggle caption, the already-muted caption, the new explainer lines, the Retry spoken label.\n   - Design doc says “Pantopus curator”; renamed to “From Pantopus” per the glossary; the card snapshot tests and the f9-nearby-cells-map copy must follow.\n   - Each explainer line is generated from who posted, the source feed, the real ranking reason (line 3) and the exclusion from counts, and each is enforced by code. Line 4 depends on that exclusion being live.\n   - Contract change request: SourceCaption “post source” variant, drawn without a ProvenanceMark on posts; add to the Foundations board.\n   - Assumptions: a neighbor’s header shows the city, never a street (matches f9-privacy-mirror); the Pulse card’s host tab (confirm before sending; do not move Pantopus content onto Today without confirmation); the 7-day review time needs product confirmation.\n   - Flag, do not redesign: whether the existing system card shows a human-looking byline.\n   - Open questions: Does the mute also filter the Pulse card and the map callout? Where does the reporter see the outcome of the review beyond “Reported · reviewing”?\n   - Omitted states: web and Android explainer expanded, same as iOS frame 3. List any others.\n\nBATCH PLAN: Turn 1: artboards 1-6, then wait for “continue”. Turn 2: artboards 7-12, then wait for “continue”. Turn 3: artboards 13-18.",
            "changeLog": [
              "Explainer lines rewritten so they answer why this post is shown (location reason in line 3) and no longer repeat 'not a neighbor'; three-line inset kept; Notes record line 3 comes from the real ranking reason (review major).",
              "Mute scope stated in a toggle caption: hides Pantopus posts in the Nearby feed and map, Saved posts keep them; mute behaviour defined for the feed, the Pulse card and post detail; open question on filtering Pulse and the map callout (review major).",
              "Collapse control renamed 'Show less' with spoken label 'Close explanation' (review major).",
              "SourceCaption no-mark usage declared as a proposed 'post source' variant and a contract change request on Notes (review major and minor).",
              "Pulse card no longer placed on the Today tab; referenced by its attached screenshot, host tab listed as an assumption; frame 11 now stacked instead of side by side (review minors).",
              "iOS overflow is a '…' button opening a pull-down menu; long-press not used (review minor).",
              "Focus moves to Undo after a mute, scroll position kept, chip carries expanded/collapsed state (review minor).",
              "Reported copy changed to 'We'll review it by Mon 26 Oct (in 7 days) and tell you here.'; persistent 'Reported · reviewing' caption added and drawn in frame 9; 7-day time flagged for product confirmation (review minors).",
              "Rows with no author record have no 'Why am I seeing this?' item; frame 7 shows it (review minor).",
              "Notes record the 'Pantopus curator' to 'From Pantopus' rename and its downstream effects (review minor).",
              "Omitted states listed: web and Android explainer expanded (review minor).",
              "Added Retry spoken label for the mute failure and the Saved posts edge case."
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "f9-earn-removal",
            "name": "Earn entries removed + a calm landing for old links",
            "platforms": [
              "web",
              "ios",
              "android"
            ],
            "isNew": false,
            "frameCount": 19,
            "promptText": "Use the Pantopus house style pasted above and the Foundations components from prompt 00, by exact name.\n\nSCREEN: Mailbox drawer, Today status strip and old Earn links · f9-earn-removal\n\nTYPE: EXTENSION of three existing designed screens: “Mailbox drawer” (the mailbox left nav on web 1440), the Today status strip (the strip at the top of the Today tab in the attached screenshot) and “Earn Wallet”. This screen already exists in the Pantopus design system. The attached screenshot is exact. Keep everything and change only what is listed:\n1. The Earn section and its row appear only for accounts that have Earn history (money available or already paid).\n2. The Today status strip loses its offers chip. For everyone without Earn history it is gone; for people with Earn history it becomes a neutral wallet chip.\n3. Old Earn links land on the Mail root with one neutral line.\n4. The wallet stays open, read-only, for people with Earn history.\n\nATTACH: the mailbox nav on web 1440 and the drawer on web 390, iOS and Android, each with its Earn section · the Today status strip with its offers chip (web Today tab; iOS; Android) · the Earn Wallet (web and iOS) · the Mail root on web 390 and iOS, including the existing mailbox-arrival pill · the existing support or contact screen.\n\nPLATFORMS & VIEWPORTS: Web 1440x900 (left sidebar plus the mailbox nav) and 390x844 (bottom tab bar, drawer). iOS 393x852 (native drawer as in the screenshot). Android 412x915 (Material 3 navigation drawer).\n\nWHERE IT LIVES & HOW PEOPLE ARRIVE\n- Mail tab → mailbox drawer, Earn section.\n- Today tab → the status strip at the top.\n- Old links: /app/mailbox/earn, /app/mailbox/earn/wallet and pantopus://mailbox/earn, from bookmarks, shared URLs and pushes sent before this change.\nAn old link hands over only the destination. Eligibility decides the landing:\n- An account with at least one Earn transaction that is available or paid goes to the wallet.\n- Everyone else goes to the Mail root.\n- While eligibility is unknown, the screen holds: under 1s, show nothing new; after 1s, show the Mail root with the Mail tab selected and a WarmingSkeleton row list, with no landing line and no Earn row. Then either land on the wallet or show the line.\n- A signed-out person goes through sign-in, which keeps the destination, and then continues to the same decision.\nNo journey in the flow spec passes through here.\n\nWHO AND WHEN: It is Mon 19 Oct 2026, 6:10 PM. Maya Chen at HOME A never earned anything. She taps an old September bookmark to /app/mailbox/earn. Sam Ortega, a member of HOME A, earned $18.50 this summer. He is the exception: he opens the drawer on Android and still finds his wallet.\n\nTHE ONE JOB: Take the cash-Earn promise off every surface for people who never earned, without hiding money from the few who did.\n\nFIRST FIVE SECONDS\n- Drawer: an ordinary list with nothing that looks missing.\n- Landing: the eye goes first to the Mail tab selected, second to the one neutral line, third to the mailbox list. The landing is already the Mail tab, so the only action on the line is “Hide this”.\n- Wallet: first the balance, second the history, third the footer and its one text button.\n\nCONTENT (house style fixtures; only the deltas are listed)\n- Drawer for most people: the rows in the screenshot minus the Earn section. The expected rows are Mail Day · Map · Vacation hold · Stamps · Unboxing · Vault · Records · Settings. If the screenshot differs, keep the screenshot and note the difference.\n- Drawer for someone with Earn history: the same rows, plus the section header “Earn” and the row “Earn Wallet” with the balance trailing (“$18.50” for Sam, “$0.00” for someone fully paid out).\n- Today status strip before (removed): “3 mail pieces waiting” · “Recycling and garbage tomorrow” · “2 offers available”.\n- After, most people: “3 mail pieces waiting” · “Recycling and garbage tomorrow”.\n- After, has Earn history (Sam): “3 mail pieces waiting” · “Recycling and garbage tomorrow” · “Earn Wallet · $18.50”, which opens the read-only wallet. It uses the same neutral chip style as the others: no offer count, no money glyph, no highlight.\n- Old-link landing: the Mail root with the Mail tab selected. The line is a LandingBannerSlot in the proposed “old Earn link” variant: a neutral 16pt info glyph, the sentence “Earn isn’t part of Pantopus right now. Everything else in your mailbox is where you left it.” and the trailing text button “Hide this”.\n- Wallet, read-only (Sam): title “Earn Wallet”, then:\n  - balance “$18.50”;\n  - “Mail scan bonus · Fri 14 Aug · $6.00”;\n  - “Referral bonus · Thu 2 Jul · $12.50”;\n  - footer “Your $18.50 stays in your account. Payouts are paused for now.”;\n  - one text button, “Ask about your balance”, which opens the existing support or contact screen.\n  No offers, no “earn more” and no withdraw button.\n- Wallet, fully paid out: balance “$0.00”, history rows such as “Referral bonus · Tue 9 Jun · $10.00 · Paid”, the footer “Your past Earn payments are listed here.” and no button.\n- Eligibility check failed on an old link: the Mail root with InlineErrorRow “We couldn’t check your Earn history. Retry”.\n- Worst case: a balance of “$1,204.75” with 40 history rows and the footer “Your $1,204.75 stays in your account. Payouts are paused for now.”, and a drawer with the Earn section and all 8 other rows at AX5.\n\nLAYOUT & VISUALIZATION\nDraw the drawer in both variants side by side, on one shared baseline grid, aligned row for row. Label them above the frames (not in the UI) “Most people” and “Has Earn history, the exception”. The rows below Earn move up by exactly one section height. Leave nothing behind:\n- no divider stranded above Settings;\n- no 48px gap;\n- no “Earn” header with nothing under it;\n- no stale badge.\n\nThe Today status strip reflows: two chips share the strip’s full width, or the native strip scrolls as the screenshot shows. There is never a hole or a ghost chip. In frame 2, draw three strips stacked, each with a visible label above it outside the UI: “Before (removed)”, “After, most people”, “After, has Earn history (Sam)”.\n\nThe landing is the ordinary Mail root. The LandingBannerSlot takes the pill geometry of the existing mailbox-arrival pill, in its position. Its sentence is text.primary with no semantic fill, never text.muted. It is not an error banner, not red and not a notice card. Exactly one banner shows: for this one landing, the old-Earn-link line ranks above mailbox arrival, and the mailbox-arrival pill waits until the next visit.\n\nThe wallet keeps its existing layout, with the history as plain rows, the footer as a caption in text.secondary, and “Ask about your balance” as a 44pt text button under the footer.\n\nINTERACTION, MOTION & HAPTICS\n- While eligibility loads, draw the most-people drawer and the most-people strip. For an eligible person the Earn section and the wallet chip are inserted without animation once the answer arrives, and only while the drawer is closed. If the drawer is already open, the section appears the next time the drawer opens.\n- The landing line fades in once over 200ms and is static under Reduce Motion.\n- “Hide this” removes the line in place with a 150ms collapse (a cross-fade under Reduce Motion). It dismisses the line for good on this account; later old-link arrivals land on the Mail root with no line.\n- “Ask about your balance” opens the existing support or contact screen; back returns to the wallet.\n- Retry refetches in place.\n- The wallet rows are not tappable unless they already are in the screenshot.\n- No haptics.\n- The drawer opens by its menu button, and its existing edge swipe is an extra.\n\nFOUNDATIONS COMPONENTS USED: LandingBannerSlot (proposed “old Earn link” variant: neutral glyph, text.primary sentence, no semantic fill, trailing action “Hide this”; ranked above mailbox arrival for this landing) · InlineErrorRow (provider-unreachable variant for the old-link check: no errorBg fill, the error hue on the glyph only, text in text.primary, Retry button; and the fail-closed rule: the Earn row renders nothing when the lookup fails) · WarmingSkeleton (Mail root row list during the old-link hold; never used for the Earn row) · OfflineNotice with FreshnessLine (offline variant).\n\nACCESSIBILITY\n- The drawer is announced with its real count: 8 items for most people, 9 for someone with Earn history.\n- The landing line is a polite status message, announced once. “Hide this” has the spoken label “Hide note about Earn”.\n- Retry has the spoken label “Retry Earn history”.\n- The wallet chip on the strip is read as “Earn Wallet, 18 dollars 50 cents”.\n- Wallet reading order: balance → history, as a list read as “Mail scan bonus, Friday 14 August, 6 dollars” → footer → “Ask about your balance”. The list announces its count (2 items for Sam, 40 in the worst case).\n- Targets: 44pt on iOS, 48dp on Android with 8dp gaps, 44px on web.\n- Text contrast is 4.5:1 or better, and no state relies on colour.\n- At AX5, amounts drop below their labels, drawer rows wrap, and the landing line wraps to several lines with “Hide this” below it.\n\nCOPY: Use every string above, plus these:\n- Offline, for someone with Earn history: FreshnessLine “You’re offline · as of 5:52 PM” above the cached wallet; “Ask about your balance” is disabled with “Contacting us needs a connection.”\n- Offline, on an old link while eligibility is unknown: show the cached Mail root with FreshnessLine “You’re offline · as of 5:52 PM”, and hold the Earn line until eligibility is known.\nNo upsell, no gating copy, no “coming back”, and no promise of future earnings.\n\nEDGE CASES\n- Eligibility loading: the most-people drawer, with no skeleton row held for Earn.\n- Old link, eligibility slow: the hold described above; never a full-page spinner.\n- Lookup failed: hide the row and the wallet chip, and show no error in the drawer.\n- Offline with a cached Today payload that still carries the offers item: show no offers chip. The strip follows the eligibility answer, not the cache.\n- Long balances and 40 history rows scroll.\n- Someone whose Earn money was all paid out sees balance “$0.00”, their history, and the footer “Your past Earn payments are listed here.”\n- A household member and an owner are treated the same: eligibility belongs to the person, not the home.\n- Signed-out: sign-in, then the same landing.\n\nINSTEAD OF\n- Instead of a 404 or a “no longer available” page, land on the Mail root with one neutral LandingBannerSlot line — because a 404 on a link the app sent last week reads as a broken product.\n- Instead of a greyed or disabled Earn row, remove the section and close the gap — because an inert row promises something that isn’t there.\n- Instead of a skeleton row while eligibility loads, draw the drawer without Earn — because a row that flashes and vanishes looks broken.\n- Instead of “coming back soon” or “Earn has closed”, write “Earn isn’t part of Pantopus right now” — because it is true today and promises nothing.\n- Instead of hiding the wallet from everyone, or a wallet that only says payouts are paused, keep it read-only with “Ask about your balance” — because locking someone out of their own money with no next step is the worst outcome here.\n- Instead of “2 offers available” for Sam, draw “Earn Wallet · $18.50” — because nothing may invite anyone to earn, but Sam’s money must stay reachable.\n- Instead of the neutral line when the check failed, show the provider-unreachable InlineErrorRow with Retry, never a red save-failed row — because this person may hold a balance, nothing was written, and an old link never shows a red state.\n- Instead of the landing line stacked under the mailbox-arrival pill, show one banner only — because the contract never allows two banners.\n\nDONE WHEN\n- Placed side by side, the two drawers differ by exactly one section and nothing else.\n- The strip never shows an offers chip to anyone, including offline; Sam sees only the neutral wallet chip.\n- An old link never produces a 404, a red state, a full-page spinner or two banners.\n- Sam can still reach his $18.50 and has a next step, and a paid-out person can still see their history.\n- Nothing on any frame invites anyone to earn.\n\nARTBOARDS\n1. f9-earn-removal · web-1440 · 01-mailbox-nav-both · light: both variants side by side on one baseline grid, labelled “Most people” and “Has Earn history, the exception”.\n2. f9-earn-removal · web-1440 · 02-status-strip-before-after · light: three strips stacked with outside labels “Before (removed)”, “After, most people”, “After, has Earn history (Sam)”.\n3. f9-earn-removal · web-390 · 03-old-link-landing · light: Mail root, Mail tab selected, the old-Earn-link LandingBannerSlot with “Hide this”, no mailbox-arrival pill.\n4. f9-earn-removal · ios · 04-drawer-most-people · light: iOS drawer without Earn.\n5. f9-earn-removal · android · 05-drawer-has-history · light: Sam’s drawer with Earn Wallet $18.50.\n6. f9-earn-removal · ios · 06-wallet-read-only · light: Sam’s balance, two history rows, footer, “Ask about your balance”.\n7. f9-earn-removal · ios · 07-eligibility-loading · light: the most-people drawer, no Earn row, no skeleton.\n8. f9-earn-removal · ios · 08-old-link-holding · light: after 1s on an old link, Mail root with the Mail tab selected and a WarmingSkeleton row list, no line.\n9. f9-earn-removal · android · 09-offline-cached-strip · light: offline, the cached Today status strip with two chips and no offers chip.\n10. f9-earn-removal · web-390 · 10-check-failed-old-link · light: Mail root with the provider-unreachable InlineErrorRow and Retry, no red fill.\n11. f9-earn-removal · web-390 · 11-drawer-most-people · light: the 390 drawer without Earn.\n12. f9-earn-removal · web-390 · 12-offline-old-link · light: cached Mail root, FreshnessLine offline, no Earn line yet.\n13. f9-earn-removal · ios · 13-offline-wallet · light: Sam’s cached wallet under FreshnessLine “You’re offline · as of 5:52 PM”, “Ask about your balance” disabled with its reason.\n14. f9-earn-removal · web-1440 · 14-wallet-worst-case · light: $1,204.75 with 40 rows scrolling, its footer and button, plus an inset of the $0.00 paid-out wallet with its footer and no button.\n15. f9-earn-removal · ios · 15-ax5-landing-and-drawer · light: the landing line and the drawer with Earn at AX5.\n16. f9-earn-removal · web-1440 · 16-greyscale · light: frame 1 in greyscale.\n17. f9-earn-removal · web-1440 · 17-mailbox-nav-both · dark: the dark twin of frame 1.\n18. f9-earn-removal · ios · 18-wallet-read-only · dark: the dark twin of frame 6, with “Ask about your balance”.\n19. f9-earn-removal · web-1440 · 19-notes · light: Notes.\n   - Invented strings: “3 mail pieces waiting”, “Recycling and garbage tomorrow”, “2 offers available”, “Earn Wallet · $18.50” chip and its spoken label, the landing line, “Hide this”, “Hide note about Earn”, Sam’s $18.50 and his earnings, “Mail scan bonus · Fri 14 Aug · $6.00”, “Referral bonus · Thu 2 Jul · $12.50”, the paid-out rows, “Your $18.50 stays in your account. Payouts are paused for now.”, “Ask about your balance”, “Contacting us needs a connection.”, “Your past Earn payments are listed here.”, the check-failed line, $1,204.75, 5:52 PM.\n   - Any drawer rows that differ from the screenshot.\n   - Assumptions: “Hide this” dismissal is stored per account, server-side. “Ask about your balance” opens the existing support or contact screen.\n   - Deviations: research asked for a verb-led action back to a tab; not needed because the redirect already lands on the Mail tab, so the action is “Hide this”. The design doc keeps the inbox_offers strip item for eligible people; this design relabels it “Earn Wallet · $18.50” and routes it to the read-only wallet. The design doc specified 404 for old Earn routes; this design redirects ineligible accounts to the Mail root instead.\n   - Contract change request: LandingBannerSlot gains an “old Earn link” variant (no actor name first, text.primary, trailing “Hide this”), used on the Mail root, ranked above mailbox arrival for this landing.\n   - Engineering: the Today payload, the web mailbox nav and the iOS and Android Earn routes all gate on the same eligibility answer (an Earn transaction that is available or paid), default hidden, on both client and server. Native route entries that outlive the payload item would open a dead area. Confirm Android has the pantopus://mailbox/earn branch. The footer “Payouts are paused for now” ships only after engineering confirms the real payout status for people with a balance.\n   - Open questions: What does the eligible strip item say and where does it go (proposed above)? Does “Payouts are paused” match the real payout status?\n   - Omitted states: iOS and Android old-link landing at default size, same as frame 3; web old-link holding, same as frame 8. List any others.\n\nBATCH PLAN: Turn 1: artboards 1-6, then wait for “continue”. Turn 2: artboards 7-12, then wait for “continue”. Turn 3: artboards 13-18, then wait for “continue”. Turn 4: artboard 19.",
            "changeLog": [
              "Strip for people with Earn history decided: the offers chip becomes a neutral 'Earn Wallet · $18.50' chip to the read-only wallet; drawn as a third strip in frame 2; deviation from the design doc and open question on Notes (review major).",
              "Old-link line is now a LandingBannerSlot 'old Earn link' variant with 'Hide this', ranked above mailbox arrival so only one banner shows; 'sits directly under it' removed; contract addition on Notes (review major).",
              "Wallet no longer a dead end: footer 'Your $18.50 stays in your account. Payouts are paused for now.' plus 'Ask about your balance' in frames 6, 13, 14, 18; footer gated on engineering confirmation (review major).",
              "Old-link holding state specified (nothing under 1s, then Mail root with WarmingSkeleton rows) and added as frame 8 (review minor).",
              "'Hide this' per-account server-side dismissal recorded as an assumption (review minor).",
              "Notes record why no verb-led back-to-tab action is needed (two review minors).",
              "Frame 2 strips carry outside labels 'Before (removed)', 'After, most people', 'After, has Earn history (Sam)' (review minor).",
              "Pickup string changed to 'Recycling and garbage tomorrow' per glossary and fixture (review minor).",
              "Manifest reordered: offline wallet before worst case, AX5, greyscale and dark twins; Notes last; omitted landing and holding variants listed; now 19 frames in 4 turns (review minor).",
              "ATTACH now says '(web Today tab; iOS; Android)' instead of the internal hub route; support screen added to ATTACH (review minor)."
            ]
          }
