                    "issue": "The main agenda draws a speculative holiday move (\"likely moved to Wed 2 Jun\") for a calendar the provider has not published. The research warns against exactly this: users complain when an app shows a holiday change that never happened. The research's rule for unpublished periods is to draw the regular marks as projected, not to predict a move. The prompt also never says which mark the moved row carries. The rule \"mark follows provenance\" plus a tick would claim that Maya added a date the app guessed.",
                    "evidence": "research-findings.md:446 cites a Recycle Coach review: \"the app showed a Presidents' Day pickup two weeks away when nothing had changed\". Its implication: \"Any pickup marks after the last published city calendar (currently Dec 2026) are drawn as projected, not confirmed.\" per-surface-changes: \"Draw a real holiday move in the agenda ... with the moved row, a struck original and the holiday source.\" The prompt: \"A moved row on Wed 2 Jun and a struck Tue 1 Jun ghost labelled 'usual day'. Caption: 'Memorial Day week — likely moved to Wed 2 Jun · Projected ...'\"",
